nd_temple_complex%2C_Deoghar_04.jpg\"\n117:     ],\n118:     description: \"Baba Baidyanath Temple complex in Deoghar is a rare confluence where a sacred Shiva Jyotirlinga and a Shakti Peeth (Hridaya Peeth) reside in adjacent sanctums. The main 72-foot stone spire features a Panchasula (five-pronged trident) and is the destination for millions during the legendary 105-km barefoot Kanwar pilgrimage.\",\n119:     highlights: [\n120:       \"Ancient main stone sanctum housing the sacred Kamana Linga\",\n121:       \"Cluster of 21 auxiliary temples dedicated to diverse Vedic deities\",\n122:       \"Rare Panchasula trident crowning the stone shikhar\",\n123:       \"Epic spiritual energy during the sacred month of Shravana\"\n124:     ],\n125:     howToReach: {\n126:       byAir: \"Deoghar International Airport (DGH) (8 km from temple)\",\n127:       byTrain: \"Jasidih Junction (JSME) (8 km) - major mainline railway hub\",\n128:       byRoad: \"Direct NH routes connect Deoghar with Patna, Ranchi, Kolkata, and Bhagalpur\"\n129:     },\n130:     safety: {\n131:       precaution: \"Keep valuables secure in cloakrooms; use official digital e-pass queues during high festival periods.\",\n132:       majorWarning: \"Avoid unauthorized middlemen outside temple gates; follow official temple trust queues exclusively.\"\n133:     },\n134:     funFact: \"Unlike other Shiva temples which have a Trishula (3 prongs), Baidyanath temple has a rare Panchasula (5 prongs) atop its spire.\"\n135:   },\n136:   {\n137:     id: \"parasnath-hill\",\n138:     name: \"Parasnath Hill (Shikharji)\",\n139:     tagline: \"The supreme sanctum on Jharkhand's highest peak (1,365m)\",\n140:     category: \"Mountain Peaks\",\n141:     district: \"Giridih\",\n142:     location: \"Parasnath, Giridih District\",\n143:     coordinates: {\n144:       lat: 23.9628,\n145:       lng: 86.1333\n146:     },\n147:     elevation: \"1,365 m (4,478 ft)\",\n148:     bestTimeToVisit: \"October to March\",\n149:     timings: \"04:00 AM - 07:00 PM\",\n150:     entryFee: \"Free\",\n151:     xpReward: 300,\n152:     stampIcon: \"⛰️\",\n153:     stampColor: \"#10B981\",\n154:     stampTitle: \"SUMMIT EXPLORER\",\n155:     unlockClue: \"Ascend to the highest summit in Jharkhand where 20 Tirthankaras attained Nirvana.\",\n156:     heroImage: \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Views_of_Shikharji_on_way_to_Anantnatha_Tonk_3.jpg/1280px-Views_of_Shikharji_on_way_to_Anantnatha_Tonk_3.jpg\",\n157:     thumbnail: \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Views_of_Shikharji_on_way_to_Anantnatha_Tonk_3.jpg/1280px-Views_of_Shikharji_on_way_to_Anantnatha_Tonk_3.jpg\",\n158:     gallery: [\n159:       \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Views_of_Shikharji_on_way_to_Anantnatha_Tonk_3.jpg/1280px-Views_of_Shikharji_on_way_to_Anantnatha_Tonk_3.jpg\"\n160:     ],\n161:     description: \"Rising dramatically above the Chotanagpur Plateau to 1,365 meters, Parasnath Hill is the highest mountain summit in Jharkhand. Revered as Shri Sammed Shikharji, 20 of the 24 Jain Tirthankaras attained Moksha (salvation) here. The 27-km pilgrimage circumambulation trail leads through misty cloud forests and ancient marble shrines.\",\n162:     highlights: [\n163:       \"Highest peak in Jharkhand offering 360-degree panoramic horizons\",\n164:       \"Sacred cluster of 31 ancient Jain temples crowning ridges and peaks\",\n165:       \"27-km scenic hiking circuit through biodiverse montane forest\",\n166:       \"Sunrise viewpoint offering sea-of-clouds vistas in winter\"\n167:     ],\n168:     howToReach: {\n169:       byAir: \"Kazi Nazrul Islam Airport Durgapur (140 km) or Ranchi (160 km)\",\n170:       byTrain: \"Parasnath Railway Station (PNME) (22 km from base town Madhuban)\",\n171:       byRoad: \"Located right off Grand Trunk Road (NH-19) with frequent transport from Dhanbad and Ranchi\"\n172:     },\n173:     safety: {\n174:       precaution: \"Begin the 27-km ascent before dawn (4:00 AM) with ample water, energy snacks, and trekking sticks.\",\n175:       majorWarning: \"Respect the religious sanctuary sanctity: non-vegetarian food, alcohol, and leather items are strictly prohibited across the holy hill.\"\n176:     },\n177:     funFact: \"Twenty Jain Tirthankaras, including Parshvanatha after whom the hill is named, attained Nirvana upon these very ridges.\"\n178:   },\n179:   {\n180:     id: \"betla-national-park\",\n181:     name: \"Betla National Park\",\n182:     tagline: \"Ancient Sal forests, tigers & 16th-century Chero forts\",\n183:     category: \"Wildlife & Nature\",\n184:     district: \"Latehar\",\n185:     location: \"Palamu District border, 160 km from Ranchi\",\n186:     coordinates: {\n187:       lat: 23.8824,\n188:       lng: 84.1887\n189:     },\n190:     elevation: \"350 m\",\n191:     bestTimeToVisit: \"November to April\",\n192:     timings: \"06:00 AM - 05:30 PM (Safari slots apply)\",\n193:     entryFee: \"₹100 per person + Safari charges\",\n194:     xpReward: 250,\n195:     stampIcon: \"🐅\",\n196:     stampColor: \"#EF4444\",\n197:     stampTitle: \"JUNGLE TRAILBLAZER\",\n198:     unlockClue: \"Explore the ancient Chero forts buried deep inside tiger territory.\",\n199:     heroImage: \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Entrance_of_Betla_national_park.jpg/1280px-Entrance_of_Betla_national_park.jpg\",\n200:     thumbnail: \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Entrance_of_Betla_national_park.jpg/1280px-Entrance_of_Betla_national_park.jpg\",\n201:     gallery: [\n202:       \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Entrance_of_Betla_national_park.jpg/1280px-Entrance_of_Betla_national_park.jpg\"\n203:     ],\n204:     description: \"One of the earliest national parks in India to come under Project Tiger in 1974, Betla spans rolling hills, teak groves, and dense sal forests. Deep inside the jungle stand the mysterious twin 16th-century Palamu Forts built by the sovereign Chero kings, blending royal Mughal-tribal stone architecture with untamed wilderness.\",\n205:     highlights: [\n206:       \"Elephant-back and 4x4 open safari tracks\",\n207:       \"Historic 16th-century Chero Dynasty jungle forts\",\n208:       \"Diverse wildlife: Elephants, leopards, gaur (Indian bison), chital & sloth bears\",\n209:       \"Lush tropical deciduous vegetation and natural waterholes\"\n210:     ],\n211:     howToReach: {\n212:       byAir: \"Ranchi Birsa Munda Airport (165 km)\",\n213:       byTrain: \"Daltonganj Railway Station (25 km)\",\n214:       byRoad: \"Regular private and state buses run from Ranchi, Daltonganj, and Netarhat\"\n215:     },\n216:     safety: {\n217:       precaution: \"Always hire a registered forest department naturalist guide and stay strictly inside safari vehicles on track routes.\",\n218:       majorWarning: \"Do not venture into fort ruins on foot without forest rangers; wild elephants and sloth bears frequent the forest corridor after sunset.\"\n219:     },\n220:     funFact: \"Betla's name is an acronym: B-Bison, E-Elephant, T-Tiger, L-Leopard, A-Axis axis (Chital).\"\n221:   }\n222: ];\n223: \n224: // Curated Informational Micro-Moments (\"Did You Know?\" editorial facts)\n225: const DID_YOU_KNOW_FACTS = [\n226:   {\n227:     number: \"01\",\n228:     tag: \"ETYMOLOGY & NATURE\",\n229:     headline: \"Jharkhand literally means 'The Territory of Forests'\",\n230:     body: \"Derived from 'Jhar' (dense forest/bushland) and 'Khand' (land), over 29% of the state's geographical area is enveloped in primeval Sal, Mahua, and Teak woodland canopy.\"\n231:   },\n232:   {\n233:     number: \"02\",\n234:     tag: \"INDIGENOUS HERITAGE\",\n235:     headline: \"Home to 32 Unique Indigenous Tribal Communities\",\n236:     body: \"Rich in living ancestral wisdom, Jharkhand's Santhal, Munda, Oraon, Ho, and Birhor tribes maintain continuous thousand-year traditions in harmony with Mother Nature (Marang Buru).\"\n237:   },\n238:   {\n239:     number: \"03\",\n240:     tag: \"LIVING ART FORMS\",\n241:     headline: \"GI-Tagged Sohrai & Khovar Murals Painted with Natural Earth\",\n242:     body: \"Tribal women paint intricate ritual wall art using naturally sourced manganese black, ochre yellow, and white kaolin clay, a tradition dating back to prehistoric rock art.\"\n243:   },\n244:   {\n245:     number: \"04\",\n246:     tag: \"GEOLOGICAL WONDERS\",\n247:     headline: \"The Chotanagpur Plateau is over 2.5 Billion Years Old\",\n248:     body: \"Part of the ancient Gondwanaland supercontinent, this dramatic plateau features stepped knickpoints creating dozens of pristine waterfalls cascading across ancient granite.\"\n249:   }\n250: ];\n251: \n252: // Travel Themes for Experience Showcase\n253: const TRAVEL_THEMES = [\n254:   {\n255:     id: \"waterfalls\",\n256:     title: \"Roaring Waterfalls & Canyons\",\n257:     subtitle: \"From 98-meter thunderous plunges to serene emerald pools\",\n258:     count: \"Hundru & Beyond\",\n259:     image: \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Hundru_Falls%2C_Jharkhand%2C_India_4.jpg/1280px-Hundru_Falls%2C_Jharkhand%2C_India_4.jpg\"\n260:   },\n261:   {\n262:     id: \"wilderness\",\n263:     title: \"Untamed Wildlife & Sal Forests\",\n264:     subtitle: \"Asia's densest Sal reserves, wild elephant herds and tigers\",\n265:     count: \"Betla Reserve\",\n266:     image: \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Entrance_of_Betla_national_park.jpg/1280px-Entrance_of_Betla_national_park.jpg\"\n267:   },\n268:   {\n269:     id: \"spiritual\",\n270:     title: \"Sacred Summits & Ancient Shrines\",\n271:     subtitle: \"Revered Jyotirlingas, Jain mountain peaks, and timeless pilgrimages\",\n272:     count: \"Baidyanath & Shikharji\",\n273:     image: \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Baidyanath_temple_and_temple_complex%2C_Deoghar_04.jpg/1280px-Baidyanath_temple_and_temple_complex%2C_Deoghar_04.jpg\"\n274:   },\n275:   {\n276:     id: \"scenic\",\n277:     title: \"Serpentine Ghats & Misty Valleys\",\n278:     subtitle: \"Patratu hairpin roads, panoramic reservoir horizons\",\n279:     count: \"Patratu Valley\",\n280:     image: \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Patratu_Valley_at_night.jpg/1280px-Patratu_Valley_at_night.jpg\"\n281:   }\n282: ];\n283: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
